WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.340
Agents made an

00:00:01.175 --> 00:00:02.472
FPGA run an

00:00:02.414 --> 00:00:03.864
LLM thirty-three times

00:00:03.675 --> 00:00:04.406
faster

00:00:04.926 --> 00:00:06.105
Ankit Gupta had

00:00:05.917 --> 00:00:07.190
never programmed an

00:00:07.025 --> 00:00:07.933
FPGA

00:00:08.110 --> 00:00:08.935
so he let

00:00:08.747 --> 00:00:09.914
coding agents write

00:00:09.726 --> 00:00:11.069
the C++ and

00:00:10.893 --> 00:00:12.107
Verilog to run

00:00:11.919 --> 00:00:13.204
an LLM on

00:00:13.016 --> 00:00:14.701
an AWS F2

00:00:14.513 --> 00:00:15.315
instance

00:00:15.838 --> 00:00:17.158
An autoresearch loop

00:00:16.994 --> 00:00:17.760
then pushed it

00:00:17.572 --> 00:00:18.479
from about six

00:00:18.291 --> 00:00:19.493
tokens per second

00:00:19.364 --> 00:00:20.283
to two hundred

00:00:20.095 --> 00:00:20.790
thirteen

00:00:21.027 --> 00:00:21.981
thirty-three times

00:00:21.793 --> 00:00:22.606
the baseline

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:23.597
for about ten

00:00:23.409 --> 00:00:24.411
thousand dollars in

00:00:24.222 --> 00:00:24.977
tokens

00:00:25.502 --> 00:00:26.928
The final FPGA

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:27.707
image beat a

00:00:27.518 --> 00:00:28.910
thirty-two core Zen

00:00:28.721 --> 00:00:29.794
5 CPU

00:00:29.889 --> 00:00:30.785
running the same

00:00:30.596 --> 00:00:31.846
model exactly by

00:00:31.658 --> 00:00:32.671
one point six

00:00:32.483 --> 00:00:33.355
five times

00:00:33.403 --> 00:00:34.287
at a similar

00:00:34.122 --> 00:00:35.160
hourly price

00:00:35.694 --> 00:00:36.708
Full story on

00:00:36.519 --> 00:00:37.415
the dashboard
